{0 How stub generation works}

@author Etienne Millon

{1 The bindings module}

The goal of this document is to explain what goes on behind the scenes when
using stub generation, in particular in the {{!page-tutorial}tutorial}.

The most "interesting" part that we wrote is the module that describes our
bindings. It corresponds to the module type {!Cstubs.BINDINGS}. In the tutorial,
that {!Bindings.Generate} ({!Cstubs.BINDINGS} is a functor type, so
[Bindings.Generate] has an argument).

{1 Stub generation}

When [generator.exe] is executed, this module is passed as an argument to
{!Cstubs.write_ml} and {!Cstubs.write_c}.

Let's see in detail how this part works. {!Cstubs.BINDINGS} is the type of a
functor with input {!Ctypes.FOREIGN} and an empty output signature. This means
that all the interesting work is done as a side effect when the functor is
applied.

{!Ctypes.FOREIGN} is a DSL that represents C types and declarations. Given the
description of some C values in that format, it is possible to write some
printers that will output this description in OCaml and C formats. This is what
{!Cstubs.write_ml} and {!Cstubs.write_c} do: they output the OCaml and C sides
of a C library that exposes the values.

Let's see how it works in the context of our tutorial.

First, a C name is generated for each C function to bind. For example, the C
function [double tgamma(double)] will be wrapped into a C function
[value gamma_1_tgamma(value)].

Then, the C part generates a C function that wraps the function with the right
macro calls:

{@c[
  value gamma_1_tgamma(value x1)
  {
     double x2 = Double_val(x1);
     double x5 = tgamma(x2);
     return caml_copy_double(x5);
  }
]}

Finally, the OCaml part defines an [external] function that corresponds to that
generated C function:

{[
  external gamma_1_tgamma : float -> float = "gamma_1_tgamma"
]}

The generated OCaml part contains more code, but let's leave it here for now.

{1 Linking}

This generated OCaml code is linked with the generated C code into a library
(named [generated] in the tutorial).

To summarize, so far:

- we wrote a [bindings] library
- [bindings] has been used by ctypes to create a [generated] library

{1 One last functor application}

This is where things get really interesting.

Notice that there are two loose threads here:

- in [generated], we've defined a [gamma_1_tgamma] OCaml function, but the
  consumer of the library can not know about this generated name.
- in [bindings], we've defined some OCaml names for the result of the [foreign]
  call, but never used these for now.

Actually, these are related. In the stub generation step before,
{!Cstubs.write_ml} actually generated glue code so that the [Generated] module
conforms to the {!Ctypes.FOREIGN} module type.

In particular, this glue code ensures that [foreign "gamma"] will call
[gamma_1_tgamma].

Since [Generated] has the right module type, this explains that the contents of
[gamma.ml] in the tutorial is just:

{[
  include Bindings.Generate (Generated)
]}

It includes a [tgamma : float -> float] function, and users of the library can
call it.

{1 At runtime}

Finally, let's see what happens when a [Gamma.tgamma 4.] is evaluated.

The definition of [Gamma.tgamma] comes from the body of
[Bindings.Generate(Generated)]. In other words, it is:

{[
  open Ctypes
  open F

  let tgamma = foreign "tgamma" (double @-> returning double)
]}

Here, [F] is instanciated as [Generated]. So the definition of [foreign] is:

{[
  let foreign : type a b. string -> (a -> b) fn -> a -> b =
   fun name t ->
    match (t, name) with
    | ( Function (CI.Primitive CI.Double, Returns (CI.Primitive CI.Double)),
        "tgamma" ) ->
        gamma_1_tgamma
  (* ... *)
]}

This pattern matching happens at module load time and simplifies to:

{[
  let tgamma = gamma_1_tgamma
]}

This function is defined in the same [Generated] module as:

{[
  external gamma_1_tgamma : float -> float = "gamma_1_tgamma"
]}

So, [tgamma 4.] passes [4.] to the C function [gamma_1_tgamma].

It is available in the linked generated stubs:

{@c[
  value gamma_1_tgamma(value x1)
  {
     double x2 = Double_val(x1);
     double x5 = tgamma(x2);
     return caml_copy_double(x5);
  }
]}

Values of type [float] are passed boxed to the OCaml runtime (this [value] is a
pointer). [Double_val] returns the underlying [double] ([x2] is [4.]). The
actual function from the libc is called and the result is stored in [x5] (
[m \Gamma(4) = 6] so [x5] is [6.]). Finally, this result is returned boxed to
the OCaml runtime ([caml_copy_double] allocated a block with [Double_tag] and
stores the [6.] value in it).

Execution resumes on the OCaml side, and the return value is propagated to the
call site of [Gamma.tgamma 4.].
